    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Manuel Garcia traces its origins to mid-eighteenth century Spanish land grants awarded to colonists from Camargo, Tamaulipas, Mexico.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/manuel_garcia_texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The community of Manuel Garcia sits near the Rio Grande, which provides fertile alluvial soils that support limited agriculture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/manuel_garcia_texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Manuel Garcia developed as a rural community tied to ranching and agriculture by the late nineteenth century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/manuel_garcia_texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "La Casita was a key precursor settlement to Manuel Garcia that functioned as a residence and supply outpost for a large ranch.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/manuel_garcia_texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Manuel Garcia features a subtropical climate with intensely hot summers and an average annual temperature of around 74 degrees Fahrenheit.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/manuel_garcia_texas"
    },
    {
